# Overview
#
# Each phrase promises a change for a later version (`will be removed`,
# `in a future release`). The docs cannot keep the promise current, so
# the reader cannot tell whether the change has shipped. The issue
# tracker holds the plan, and the docs describe what ships. The fix
# describes the system as it ships, such as `the flag is deprecated`.
# The rule sits at Recommended, because a phrase can also name the
# reader's own future or a step of a tutorial.
#
# Expression
#
# Four phrases keep a runtime sense behind a guard. `in the future`
# skips a word in `when` before it: a form of `be`, `have`, or a time
# noun. It also skips a glued `?` after it. Weir has no lookbehind, so
# the filter consumes the word before `in the future` and narrows the
# finding back to the phrase.
#
# A glued comma has its own branch, so the sentence opener (`In the
# future, ...`) still counts once. That opener skips `whenever`, `when`,
# `if`, or `you` after the comma. `we` still counts there, because `In
# the future, we plan to add` is a promise. By default, a Weir match
# stops at a comma, so `let scope "Sentence"` lets the opener branch
# cross one.
#
# `will be added` skips `to` after it. A negated phrase reads ahead and
# consumes one word, so the filter takes any word after `will be` unless
# `added to` starts there, and it narrows the finding to `will be
# added`. The filter does not need a word after `added`, so the phrase
# counts before any punctuation mark and as the last words of a text
# (`Which formats will be added?`). The guard also drops `no new
# features will be added to cgroup v1`, a promise the rule misses.
#
# `is planned` skips `by` after it. A negated word after a space needs a
# word to consume, so the branch has a twin for the end of a clause. A
# clause is a subject and its verb, such as `the tests pass`. `to be
# implemented` needs `yet`, `still`, or `remains` before it.
#
# False Positives
#
# After a form of `be`, `have`, or a time noun, `in the future` carries
# a runtime sense (`the timestamp must be in the future`, `values we
# might have in the future`, `two years in the future`). A question does
# the same (`Is the date in the future?`). A tutorial uses the opener
# for the reader's own next steps (`In the future, whenever you push a
# change, the site rebuilds`).
#
# `will be added to` says what the code does with a value (`all headers
# will be added to the map`). Before `by` and its actor, `is planned`
# says what the system does (`the query is planned by the optimizer`).
# `to be implemented` alone names a capability (`allows a filesystem to
# be implemented in user space`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# ProseFutureTense skips `will be` before `supported`, `removed`, and
# `deprecated`. It skips `will be added` unless `to` follows, so it
# reports `will be added to`, the phrase that the guard here skips. Both
# tiers of ProsePassive skip `be` right after `will`. Only this rule
# then reports `will be removed`.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 94 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 14 of 20 sampled
# hits promised a later change. Most misfires were the reader's own
# future (`crates you might use in the future`) or a tutorial step
# (`Going forward, let's add the package`).
expr when [be, is, are, was, were, have, time, date, point, timestamp, deadline, cutoff, moment, instant, far, further, year, years]
expr tutorial [whenever, when, if, you]
expr main [(going forward), <(!@when in the future![,, ?]), (in the future)>, (in the future, !@tutorial), (in a future release), (in a future version), (in an upcoming release), (in upcoming releases), (in future releases), (in future versions), <(will be !(added to)), (will be added)>, (will be supported), (will be removed), (will be deprecated), ([is, are] planned !by), ([is, are] planned!( )), (on the roadmap), (coming soon), (not yet implemented), (not yet supported), ([yet, still, remains, remain] to be implemented), (will soon), (soon be)]
let message "Roadmap promise. Describe the system as it ships, such as `the flag is deprecated`, and keep plans in the issue tracker."
let description "Flags phrases that promise a change for a later version."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The parser supports tables."
allows "The timestamp must be in the future."
allows "A since cutoff in the future excludes the row."
allows "The query is planned by the optimizer."
allows "The library allows a filesystem to be implemented in user space."
allows "In the future, whenever you push a change, GitHub Pages rebuilds the site."
allows "All headers will be added to the map."
